WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.983
In App Lab, the toolbox now has a tab called Functions.

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:13.263
There are two blocks.

00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:17.423
This one defines a new function,

00:00:17.423 --> 00:00:19.967
and this one calls a function.

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:26.847
Let's make a function that updates the number of likes on the screen and plays a sound.

00:00:26.847 --> 00:00:29.951
We'll call it twice, once in each

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:32.451
onEvent block.

00:00:32.451 --> 00:00:37.091
Drag a define a function block out.

00:00:37.091 --> 00:00:39.931
Notice how both of these onEvent blocks

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:42.815
have the same last two commands in them.

00:00:45.135 --> 00:00:48.895
To help simplify this program, let's take these commands and

00:00:48.895 --> 00:00:49.895
turn them into a

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:59.799
function. And let's give the function a name like updateScreen. Now we can

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:03.423
remove the repeated code from the rest of the program.

00:01:04.903 --> 00:01:05.743
Now click Run.

00:01:08.023 --> 00:01:09.823
Hey, we defined a function,

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:11.887
but nothing happened.

00:01:11.887 --> 00:01:15.887
The reason is we forgot step two.

00:01:15.887 --> 00:01:18.887
We need to call the function.

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:27.871
To call a function, drag out the "call a function" block from the toolbox and put it in the program.

00:01:27.871 --> 00:01:29.855
Now add in the

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:31.855
name of the function we just defined.

00:01:33.355 --> 00:01:37.115
Now that we have both defined and called our function,

00:01:37.115 --> 00:01:39.839
the code will actually run, and the likes

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:41.379
will change on the screen.

00:01:41.379 --> 00:01:42.219
Check it out.

00:01:45.319 --> 00:01:47.039
It works.

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:49.799
Notice that if I also call the function

00:01:49.823 --> 00:01:53.143
before its definition, it still works.

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:04.407
[MUSIC]

00:02:04.407 --> 00:02:08.227
Defining a function simply gives a name to a block of code, but

00:02:08.227 --> 00:02:09.487
it doesn't actually run it.

00:02:09.791 --> 00:02:13.791
The definition can be anywhere in your program.

00:02:13.791 --> 00:02:17.791
This means that your code can get messy and really hard to read,

00:02:17.791 --> 00:02:19.775
so we typically put our

00:02:19.775 --> 00:02:25.695
function definitions at the end of our programs to help keep things organized.

00:02:25.695 --> 00:02:29.735
You can also see why it's important to give your function a meaningful and descriptive

00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:32.559
name. If you call it something like doStuff,

00:02:32.559 --> 00:02:35.919
it'll be unclear what the function is supposed to do,

00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:38.399
and it makes it hard to use in your own code.

00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:44.383
Giving a function a meaningful name makes it much easier to understand what it does.

00:02:44.383 --> 00:02:49.503
Using functions like this in your program not only makes your code easier to understand,

00:02:49.727 --> 00:02:53.207
it also makes it easier to fix problems or errors.

00:02:53.207 --> 00:02:55.767
When you fix a problem inside your function,

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:58.047
that will automatically fix that problem

00:02:58.047 --> 00:02:59.711
wherever the function is called.

00:02:59.711 --> 00:03:02.131
(soft music)
